"A bill of rights is a list of things the government may not do. The Constitution of 1787 had none, and that absence was one of the main objections of its opponents during ratification. James Madison, who had defended the Constitution in The Federalist, introduced amendments in the House of Representatives on June 8, 1789. Congress sent twelve to the states that September, and ten were ratified by December 1791. They are written as commands to the government. The First Amendment does not say “people have freedom of speech”; it says “Congress shall make no law … abridging the freedom of speech.” The rights are described as things the government must leave alone.": "a45856e65d0b",
"A bill of rights is a list of things the government may not do. The Constitution of 1787 had none, and that absence was one of the main objections of its opponents during ratification. James Madison, who had defended the Constitution in The Federalist, introduced amendments in the House of Representatives on June 8, 1789. Congress sent twelve to the states that September, and ten were ratified by December 1791. They are written as commands to the government. The First Amendment does not say “people have freedom of speech”; it says “Congress shall make no law … abridging the freedom of speech.” The rights are described as things the government must leave alone. The ten amendments cover several kinds of protection. The First protects religion, speech, the press, peaceful assembly, and petitions to the government. The Second is quoted in the model without discussion of its meaning, and the Third forbids quartering soldiers in private homes in peacetime without the owner's consent. The Fourth through the Eighth deal mostly with how the government may treat people it suspects or accuses of crimes: no unreasonable searches, no forcing a person to testify against himself, no trying a person twice for the same offense, a speedy and public trial by jury, the help of a lawyer, and no excessive bail or cruel and unusual punishments. The last two amendments are different. The Ninth answers a worry raised during ratification, that listing some rights might suggest that the people had no others. It says that the listing of certain rights “shall not be construed to deny or disparage others retained by the people.” The Tenth, which Lesson 3 discussed, is about powers rather than rights: what was not delegated to the United States stays with the states or the people. Together they show the theory behind the whole list. The people keep everything they did not give away, and the list marks some of the most important things the government is not allowed to touch. One point is easy to miss. The Bill of Rights restrains government, not private persons. A city that punishes a speaker, a public school that compels a student to recite a pledge, and a police officer who searches a house without a warrant are all governments acting, so the Constitution applies. A private company that fires an employee for a speech, or a newspaper that refuses to print a letter, is not the government, and the First Amendment does not reach it, although other laws may. Lawyers call this the state action requirement: a constitutional right is a claim against the state, meaning the government at any level. At first, “the government” meant only the national government. In Barron v. Baltimore in 1833, a wharf owner argued that the city of Baltimore had taken his property without compensation, contrary to the Fifth Amendment. Chief Justice John Marshall replied that the amendments limited the government of the United States and not the governments of the states. For decades afterward, a state could restrict speech or religion without violating the federal Bill of Rights, and people had to rely on their own state constitutions. That changed only after the Civil War and the abolition of slavery, when the country rewrote the relationship between the nation, the states, and the people. The Fourteenth Amendment, ratified in 1868, says that no state shall “deprive any person of life, liberty, or property, without due process of law.” Over many decades the Supreme Court read its word “liberty” to include most of the Bill of Rights, and so applied those guarantees to the states. Lawyers call this incorporation. It happened one right at a time. The duty to pay for property taken for public use came early, in 1897. Freedom of speech followed in 1925, and most of the protections for people accused of crimes followed in the 1960s. A few provisions, such as the Third Amendment and the right to a jury in civil cases, have never been applied to the states. None of these rights is absolute, and courts decide where the limits lie. In 1943 the Supreme Court held that a public school could not force students to salute the flag. In 1969 it held that students who wore black armbands to school were protected, because students do not shed their rights “at the schoolhouse gate,” although a school may restrict speech that would materially and substantially interfere with its work. To judge such limits, courts use different standards. Under the strictest, the government must show that a law serves a compelling interest and is narrowly drawn. Under the mildest, a law needs only a reasonable connection to a legitimate purpose. Who should have the final say on the reach of these rights has been argued since 1789. Courts decide cases, but legislatures pass laws that protect rights further, and the people can amend the Constitution, as they did after the Civil War. Many questions about the Bill of Rights remain deeply contested in American public life, and this series does not take sides on them. What it can show is the framework: a written list of limits on government, a clause that carried those limits to the states, and a long history of courts, legislatures, and citizens arguing about what the words require.": "330fb8dca745",
